#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "a5753b43-b288-5cca-a26b-b060917182ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3330" :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2)}\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{x+y} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot {2}^{3}}{(-1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{1} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124257 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "301263ef-3b68-54ac-87aa-5964484f5a14" :question/math-academy-id "e-3331" :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 0 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124261 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "56539c63-dd81-57c9-b7d2-18c935953203" :question/math-academy-id "e-3366" :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2(-x))}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "ae167d95-9cfd-5f31-91b1-484becd62677" :question/math-academy-id "e-6239" :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{(x-2\\sqrt{y})(x+2\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-(2\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-4y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y}) & =2+2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c22d73-cf17-502a-b856-207b42d6d523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565045} {:answer/id #uuid "649ec646-7b0f-548e-9c35-08857d9dbb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565093} {:answer/id #uuid "804fbbe3-5623-55ba-bc08-0b6088ac245d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565135} {:answer/id #uuid "b490877c-e83c-59f4-a1c9-d3fa593a9f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565210} {:answer/id #uuid "ed15ba70-79a7-5e28-8379-b7e2a244dd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "804fbbe3-5623-55ba-bc08-0b6088ac245d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565135} :answer-field/id #uuid "eecb02cb-44a7-5766-9e3d-1d6dd4f8028f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6491bac7-604f-53bc-80e6-378575a48cb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-129324" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x-y+3.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-2,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3) & =2(-2)-0+3 \\\\  & =-4-0+3 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da8b2db2-4123-5665-b738-98ed34b75b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378624} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3687c4-99c4-5acf-aa17-8ee4a667504b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378625} {:answer/id #uuid "5cfdc49f-f710-579f-a121-34bd82cc68c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378626} {:answer/id #uuid "5bef6f1c-9c50-5077-83d6-7937a6ae0e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378627} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4dca40-4471-5b61-bf8b-e5086c017493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b4dca40-4471-5b61-bf8b-e5086c017493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378628} :answer-field/id #uuid "b89502e5-3b74-56d2-882e-fcb6a0ee1e92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88dbd745-2040-5337-99bf-90ad013cc0a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-129327" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,\\pi )$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c1cdaa-8ccc-5f2d-a004-9ae4846bab35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264272} {:answer/id #uuid "aa551ce0-e36c-55d8-b6d7-caa9fba4882b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264454} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa9a350-3a47-50f2-8f0e-6a5936d8b19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264818} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8fac4d-b063-52b0-8ed7-a78b532ee43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265043} {:answer/id #uuid "fccf2baa-e984-5f4c-9fc3-9a03dfb968c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa551ce0-e36c-55d8-b6d7-caa9fba4882b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264454} :answer-field/id #uuid "619cb15a-2c93-5319-81cd-c8a7665c35b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d53fcb5b-03e0-54f4-94bd-b9eac7281e74" :question/math-academy-id "q-129329" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1} & =\\sqrt[3]{3(1)-2(-2)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0569550f-51bd-575d-b80b-ec093b07682c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263049} {:answer/id #uuid "298d83f3-e1e3-5bce-96c8-4b0b8973f332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263357} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0c56eb-cdff-5db9-9329-b25d29d16a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264610} {:answer/id #uuid "ba22f1e6-506d-59b4-8ad2-edde9f213b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264611} {:answer/id #uuid "e27a6e9e-7126-5be8-b5d1-8378b185a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e27a6e9e-7126-5be8-b5d1-8378b185a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264967} :answer-field/id #uuid "a66c8923-8d97-5e59-b5ab-27bce45dda8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa92e0c7-08cd-5cb2-af58-76718486c28e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129339" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x-y)}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)\\cancel{(x-y)}}{x\\cancel{(x-y)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x} & =\\frac{-1+(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f90210-da8d-511b-9d38-b3d158c315d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407827} {:answer/id #uuid "4005330f-5c8b-5294-9f96-5e9be2626147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} {:answer/id #uuid "88b04b8f-a6d9-5b86-ba06-a0eb6a004245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407833} {:answer/id #uuid "b72aa7bf-e6dc-52f1-8358-bfb499369454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407838} {:answer/id #uuid "d17e2afc-964e-5d9b-b663-5d2974b50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4005330f-5c8b-5294-9f96-5e9be2626147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} :answer-field/id #uuid "e093674e-caf5-5f8d-97ff-ca3172fa6889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fac05de1-cec1-5741-a5ec-beb8567ada24" :question/math-academy-id "q-129343" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\frac{1+(-1)}{\\sqrt{2-1}-\\sqrt{2+(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z})(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(x-y)-(x+z)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{-(y+z)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(y+z)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{\\cancel{(y+z)}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z}) & =-(\\sqrt{2-1}+\\sqrt{2+(-1)}) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0baa5e10-5459-5732-9dd7-5d51a31a7b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263111} {:answer/id #uuid "3fec9ee5-6ae2-58fe-8a38-325b070dba28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263564} {:answer/id #uuid "815895b6-8daa-5f01-b1e0-50eb24942d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264112} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0f99ee-d7bb-5f69-b6bb-53b9408bee54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265068} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd17ed5-cd78-5b95-8110-e650724142fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed0f99ee-d7bb-5f69-b6bb-53b9408bee54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265068} :answer-field/id #uuid "f643f621-c0dd-5dc1-b633-084448f646c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43a8fc7d-552c-5ee1-ac4a-431ddf37f045" :question/math-academy-id "q-129345" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & =\\frac{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}-1}{({1}^{2})(2+(-1))-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{(x\\sqrt{y+z}-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)}(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36de98d3-7bb5-5915-95e1-b5b78a147e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263490} {:answer/id #uuid "41df6099-6368-5629-97e7-c9681245feb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263583} {:answer/id #uuid "a9fbcaf4-6b4a-5eed-b561-6f65f5756d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264452} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ae1d76-9dd1-56fd-88c0-3619b135a8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264554} {:answer/id #uuid "e08b40a4-9f01-5372-973d-b6907da00dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e08b40a4-9f01-5372-973d-b6907da00dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264949} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df59ef6-6f84-55da-879a-62c922b8039c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "98eb7cb6-6c4e-52ce-9f7d-3ac96c9eabeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-129346" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & =\\frac{1+0-{1}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{(\\sqrt{x+y}-z)(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{x+y-{z}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x+y-{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{\\cancel{x+y-{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z) & =\\sqrt{1+0}+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f47eef-aa78-50a5-b976-e93df5f4c292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564942} {:answer/id #uuid "41b96b77-5e73-5c67-af13-27ac8b7eb13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565034} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a30f22-3124-51ba-913f-5a068a49c15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565237} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bef289-3178-5ae8-94bb-0bc7078744bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565261} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c561d0-62a5-54e4-bf55-93094dadec30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41b96b77-5e73-5c67-af13-27ac8b7eb13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565034} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3985035-b9b2-56db-8d94-fdc34f531158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbddc293-6a3c-5b64-88ba-c447f360dc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129347" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\frac{9-({1}^{2})({3}^{2})}{\\sqrt{9}-1(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-9}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & ={lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{(\\sqrt{x}-yz)(\\sqrt{x}+yz)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{\\cancel{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz) & =\\sqrt{9}+1(3) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed9a8298-54cd-5904-87cc-0773ceed9484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378638} {:answer/id #uuid "16292b9f-eddf-569d-8d1e-2dd760b68415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378639} {:answer/id #uuid "40076bfe-6573-5b16-91f1-cdb51a556125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378640} {:answer/id #uuid "47ea9746-2601-59cf-97ee-1ee69879d0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378641} {:answer/id #uuid "6cca0148-f328-5676-8042-40e9ef488295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40076bfe-6573-5b16-91f1-cdb51a556125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f471c6a4-8548-5953-bc64-722a32227684" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a45c5a39-df0e-5ca7-830f-279fde3ab785" :question/math-academy-id "q-129403" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c03dc64-0f3b-5e8f-a8ea-480cf8c23bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122223} {:answer/id #uuid "c892c477-c790-5a52-9139-e23e36c04a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122224} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac772a4-2c3f-5016-95a7-5056a887f722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122225} {:answer/id #uuid "577e0ce2-bfb6-5fd9-aa5b-620334d6829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122226} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6d19bd-365e-5681-9a90-a675402ec3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c03dc64-0f3b-5e8f-a8ea-480cf8c23bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122223} :answer-field/id #uuid "71ff1c87-4a2b-5ff3-8f60-90ba048bfa23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f613246-2fd9-5309-ad22-34ad0114450f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35952" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}+{1}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5493b42d-db22-5575-bb2f-f6c8fc5d6eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321811} {:answer/id #uuid "558ecdfc-8fe6-5e98-a11d-9431a5f8ca70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321824} {:answer/id #uuid "db11aabe-6fb7-5f73-bda7-0a282cd8baf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322863} {:answer/id #uuid "f53896e5-4b71-5c33-b0fd-046da56c652b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323070} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbdb260-48f2-5b5b-b5cd-96873b9883f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5493b42d-db22-5575-bb2f-f6c8fc5d6eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321811} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c09b5db-1f6a-58bf-8c80-c3937e3dcaaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ae648fa-9940-58c2-8b0b-e34933c46f7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36109" :question/problem "${lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first factoring the denominator, and then canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy} & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xy-xy-2{y}^{2}}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2x(x+2y)-y(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x} & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot 2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c4d527-5e01-5264-8001-c16ba5c829f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453880} {:answer/id #uuid "8a788be2-6daa-5a9a-b61f-b090c8b8cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a788be2-6daa-5a9a-b61f-b090c8b8cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453881} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d46ebdc-4ea8-5b5d-a791-12ebf82b2b2b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7662fdd-d050-5004-a857-2d86c30b4a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453882} {:answer/id #uuid "9233290a-d139-5d2d-91b5-3115178cf2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7662fdd-d050-5004-a857-2d86c30b4a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453882} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9a1015-1583-5e39-b729-2685f362e667" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f5a5f0-e3d5-58cb-a9a2-e0e244128177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453884} {:answer/id #uuid "6013b3d6-d8f8-5f12-8351-83ccb30ce208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f5a5f0-e3d5-58cb-a9a2-e0e244128177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453884} :answer-field/id #uuid "81040326-5134-5a42-8539-aac88ecd9a5e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9a907de-0404-5ffb-8362-e9676298070b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36139" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- **II.** The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$\n- **III.** The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116e62d7-9676-5ce3-b68b-c1b2b15289ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564966} {:answer/id #uuid "1c785494-6256-5978-8b37-733bf1a00a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564980} {:answer/id #uuid "55deb21b-ba9f-58dd-91dc-0aad1e32f9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565065} {:answer/id #uuid "b04bbf06-0a8f-5897-8e52-3272aed01886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565200} {:answer/id #uuid "f3957818-b350-5503-95cc-19f562857757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3957818-b350-5503-95cc-19f562857757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565306} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ebaa1a-8a6b-5d7a-acd1-3db045338942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec0d423c-ac59-5358-a37f-88f2016b24ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-65138" :question/problem "${lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{(-1)^{3}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy+xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x+y)+y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)+y(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(2x+y)(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y} & =\\frac{2(-1)+(1)}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52b7df89-d99c-5b42-9cc2-063604231fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342929} {:answer/id #uuid "8e43633a-b13b-5b08-9991-4a855a976614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343484} {:answer/id #uuid "be0eb3e2-b2bb-5b4d-8e0b-d09e4ab2c128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343913} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8ebb93-6c86-56d6-af46-1bb108780271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344050} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a1e3df-1ed6-57d4-81e0-257ba2a81c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52b7df89-d99c-5b42-9cc2-063604231fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342929} :answer-field/id #uuid "a81e50a8-3dec-549b-a0cf-3a09d9dfb58b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f277d23-3dcb-54b8-9f1f-cefa2a015d92" :question/math-academy-id "q-74912" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So,  we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,4)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot 4}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "392ee925-0a86-58e9-be9d-088d11882899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380057} {:answer/id #uuid "95b9f301-3e4c-5b4a-8f90-c92aeb09e382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380058} {:answer/id #uuid "988389d2-83d7-50e4-b103-318efb7628de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380059} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0a15c7-e641-5ba1-805d-360eea7a6554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380060} {:answer/id #uuid "b8655f26-6734-5a2e-bdb9-ca9cf96b1f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f0a15c7-e641-5ba1-805d-360eea7a6554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380060} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e2ee50-e13c-591e-b10c-41485a4529eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "142437af-f631-571c-967f-34ef02138536" :question/math-academy-id "q-74919" :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0\\cdot 2}{{0}^{3}-2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x(x-y)}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2} & =\\frac{0-2}{0+2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b854f763-ed80-5765-977e-d777be9389dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123939} {:answer/id #uuid "b9820c9d-fbc4-5ec7-8e94-b92be2bcca27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123940} {:answer/id #uuid "bb91eaa9-b9b0-507a-a5d1-1276815c0414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123941} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8208af-32ab-5380-8ca8-41fb48e098cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123942} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9a7dee-ade9-57bb-b47b-7818602e9219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9a7dee-ade9-57bb-b47b-7818602e9219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123943} :answer-field/id #uuid "406cbd00-ec1e-58cf-b40f-a0b22e94c3e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e3fea14-2815-5683-ac38-ab51981e0e10" :question/math-academy-id "q-74924" :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot {2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz(x-y)}{-(x-y)(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{-2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48848976-5af9-59a1-a8d5-7f34db74d877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565043} {:answer/id #uuid "77866715-4a00-54a6-9bca-78618431fd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565118} {:answer/id #uuid "984fc114-e07e-535b-bbfd-cf1625fa9db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565168} {:answer/id #uuid "c27ee232-913c-501f-8f38-24c7f5cfaae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565232} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d96702-504b-5f4f-9d1f-1e62ddfecd8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77866715-4a00-54a6-9bca-78618431fd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565118} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c29978b-66bd-5fe3-92c9-25071bfcad0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e84a1e0-ab07-582e-a187-ce57adaa478b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74961" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{1-3+2}{\\sqrt{3-2}-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x-z})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{-(x-z-y)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{3-2}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3baf4567-d424-573a-9e30-4ff96fa2d669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342729} {:answer/id #uuid "4f893e89-6a93-53ab-b746-44f7cf20c423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342900} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5dd490-0f0f-53bd-b098-70c3ce3d0f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343196} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9e3fe9-3652-5268-9ac3-a883593c3468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343618} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f15882-a4eb-5654-8f46-170a82fde8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f15882-a4eb-5654-8f46-170a82fde8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343658} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7ab88d0-e46a-5e99-86e7-68aecdee4f07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "56c07c72-df51-5ec4-88ac-9185b7690371" :question/math-academy-id "q-74966" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{3}}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2776b54f-1b5d-5a24-9264-60188b98a30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565001} {:answer/id #uuid "47a3084a-dea7-5252-a052-f22a8d9afbb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565041} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2058cc-d3ee-5a9d-abaa-0c2aab0fc895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565157} {:answer/id #uuid "91e130a9-184e-5357-91fa-a31e71ffed64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565159} {:answer/id #uuid "9b09b8cb-03b3-5495-a712-64af8592a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2776b54f-1b5d-5a24-9264-60188b98a30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565001} :answer-field/id #uuid "a63c1194-144a-5227-ac9c-0935b59c5eb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "416deefa-43e7-520e-bca5-f8da057711d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74967" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=0$ (the $x$-axis),  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c334b8-5f64-5024-8be8-3440026a135f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564995} {:answer/id #uuid "b3785525-f411-5114-943e-5a2cf050adfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565205} {:answer/id #uuid "b689f50d-16ad-525a-aff0-c88e838473ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565212} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cccb62-ae0c-505e-946a-32fd9dfe1c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565241} {:answer/id #uuid "d4522578-3775-5aa6-9639-2a945876ec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7cccb62-ae0c-505e-946a-32fd9dfe1c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565241} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1873304-acce-598c-9592-a96cff0a14c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "585a757a-6bf9-546a-9bf9-80289d848f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-74968" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=-x$,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,(-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}]}
